-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Contents
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The contents of this package are as follows.

README.en: This file
README: Readme file written in Japanese
CHANGELOG: Changelog (in Japanese)
COPYRIGHT: Copyright statement
LGPL: GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL)
Makefile: Main part of the Makefile
Mkinclude: Include file for the Makefile (The default settings are suitable
   for most for Linux distributions)
Mkinclude.sub: Include file for Makefiles for each of 
   the following subdirectories

appack: Time measure routine
bspack: Lower level routines such as array copying
fepack: Upper level I/O routines (independent of machine type)
fhpack: Lower level I/O routines (simple I/O routines)
flpack: Filter routines such as converting the binary representation of 
        floating point numbers
ftpack: Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) routines
p2pack: Transform routines for Double-Fourier models
pzpack: Same as p2pack except for using fftj 
p3pack: Transform routines for three dimensional models with periodic 
        boundary conditions
p3pack-mpi: Parallelized version of p3pack using MPI
c2pack: Transform routines for channel geometry models
u2pack: Transform routines for infinite y-interval models
ujpack: Same as u2pack except for using fftj 
snpack: Spherical harmonics transform routines (for vector processors)
snpack-mpi: Parallelized version of snpack using MPI
sppack: Application routines based on snpack
sppack-mpi: Parallelized version of sppack using MPI
sopack: routines to compute efficiently non-linear terms of the vorticity 
        equation of two-dimensional non-divergent flow on a rotating sphere 
sopack-mpi: Parallelized version of sopack using MPI
sjpack: Spherical harmonics transform routines (for scalar processors)
sjpack-mpi: Parallelized version of sjpack using MPI
sjpack-cuda: CUDA version of transform routines in sjpack
tdpack: Routines for time-integrations (solve ordinary differential equations)
dkpack: Routines for spectral method on a disk domain (Because this package
        uses LAPACK, you should also install LAPACK if you use this package)
sample: Sample programs using the subroutines above to conduct numerical
        experiments in fluid mechanics
.fftj:  Directory for fftj-0.2 bundled in this package. The Makefile
        in the directory is changed from the original version
       (Please refer to .fftj/README for the detail of fftj.)

 Each **pack directory has the following two subdirectories.
Please refer to them if needed.
  
  src: directory that contains sources of the subroutines
  doc: directory that contains the document of the subroutines
       (LaTex format; written in Japanese)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
How to install ISPACK library
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  1) Change macro definitions in Mkinclude suitablly for your environment.
     
    Though the meaning of each macro may be obvious, explanations are
    given only for the macros that may have to be changed.

    - DIRS: Place package names that are desired to be included in the
            library. In the default setting, all packages except for 
            sjpack-cuda are listed. If you do not need all the packages
            to be included, place the desired package names only.
            (For example, if you need IO packages only, you should set 
             as "DIRS = flpack fhpack fepack" .)

    - LIBNAME: The name of the static library to be installed. You do 
          not have to change the default value "libisp" if there is 
          no possibility to conflict with other libraries.

    - DESTDIR: The intall directory for the library.

    - FC,FFLAGS: The name of FORTRAN77 compiler and the flags (for 
         optimization, for example) for the compiler.

    - MPIFC,MPIFFLAGS: The name of FORTRAN77 compiler for MPI programs
         and the flags (for optimization, for example) for the compiler.

    - CC,CFLAGS: The name of C compiler and the flags for the compiler.

    - DCLFRT: In sample programs in the subdirectories under the "sample" 
         directory, graphical objects are drawn with dcl 
         (http://www.gfd-dennou.org/arch/dcl/).
         If you compile these sample programs, you should set the name 
         of the compiler that links the dcl library (normally, dclfrt).
         (However, dcl is not required to install ISPACK itself).

    - SSE: A switch to be set in making fftj and sjpack.
         It should be set depending on your computational environment 
         as follows:
         
      * If your system is a 32bit Linux on a x86 CPU which has SSE2 
        (after Pentium4), then set as;
	    SSE = sse32 

      * If your system is a 64bit Linux on a x86 CPU which has SSE2 
        (after Pentium4), then set as;
	    SSE = sse64

      * If your system is a 64bit Linux on a x86 CPU which has AVX
        (after Sandy Bridge), then set as;
	    SSE = avx

      * If your system is not mentioned above, then set as;
	    SSE = fort

    - CPPFLAGS:	Flags used in making IO routines, fhpack and fepack.
         Set appropriately depending on your computational environment.
         If you do not use these packages, you do not have to change 
         the default settings.

       i) Flag for IO.

	  If direct access IO is available (for example, on most UNIX 
          systems), add the following:
  	
		-DDIRECT 

	  If direct access IO is not available (for example, on mainframe
          systems), add the following:

		-DSEQUENTIAL

          If there are any problems with unformatted IO (for example,
          if you use ifort on Linux), add the following:

	        -DDIRECT -DFORMAT

       ii) Flags for the binary representation of floating point numbers.

	   Select one of the followings depending on the architecture.

	        -DIEEE (IEEE representation) or -DIBM (IBM representation)

       iii) Flags for  the binary representation of strings.
	    
	   Select one of the followings depending on the architecture.

	        -DASCII (ASCII encoding) or -DEBCDIC (EBCDIC encoding)

       iv) Flag for the endian of the system.

	   If the system is little endian, add the following:

 		-DLEN

	   If the system is big endian, this flag should not be added.
	  
       Examples for the flag settings)

	  - Using gfortran on Linux on a x86 CPU:

		CPPFLAGS = -DDIRECT -DIEEE -DASCII -DLEN 

	  - Using ifort on Linux on a x86 CPU:

		CPPFLAGS = -DDIRECT -DFORMAT -DIEEE -DASCII -DLEN 

  2) In this directory, where this README.en file is located, execute
     as follows.

	% make

     Then, the library, libisp.a, is created in the directory specified 
     as DESTDIR.

      Note: Some warning messages about grammatical violation may 
        be displayed in the make process, especially in compiling
        IO packages (fhpack, fepack, and flpack). However, they 
        usually do no harm and please ignore them.
        
  3) After making the library, if you want to remove unnecessary object 
     files, execute as follows.

	% make clean

     Furthermore, if you execute as follows,

	% make veryclean

    the library and temporary files are also removed and this directory
    is restored to the original state before the execution of make. 
    If you make a mistake in selecting CPPFLAGS, etc, please redo the
    installation from the beginning.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
How to use the library
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

 Since the library created by the above procedure (libisp.a) is a normal 
FORTRAN77 library, you can link your program with it when being compiled.
Please refer to the subdirectories under the "sample" directory to see 
example codes to show how to use.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Sample programs
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Simple sample programs based on this library for numerical experiment
of fluid mechanics are located in subdirectories under the "sample" 
directory. There are the following subdirectories so far.

 - 3D-box: Programs for numerical experiments of 3D incompressible
           fluid (3D periodic boundary condition).

 - 3D-box-mpi: Parallelized version of the programs in "3D-box" with MPI.

 - 2D-plane: Programs for numerical experiments of 2D fluid 
             (2D-plane geometry; doubly periodic boundary condition).

 - 2D-channel: Programs for numerical experiments of 2D fluid 
             (2D-channel geometry; periodic and slip boundary condition).

 - 2D-disk-inviscid-shallow: Programs for numerical experiments of 2D fluid 
             (inviscid shallow-water equation in a disk domain).

 - non-div-2d-sphere: Programs for numerical experiments of 2D non-divergent
             fluid (on a sphere).
 
 - non-div-2d-sphere-mpi: Parallelized version of the programs in 
            "non-div-2d-sphere" with MPI.
 
 - non-div-2d-sphere-sjpack: Programs for numerical experiments of 
             2D non-divergent fluid (on a sphere) implemented by sjpack.

 - shallow-water-sphere: Programs for numerical experiments of shallow-water
             equation (on a sphere).

 - shallow-water-sphere-mpi: Parallelized version of the programs in 
            "shallow-water-sphere" with MPI.

 - IO: Sample programs for IO based on f[ehl]pack.
 
 - snpack-openmp-test: Test programs for transform routines in snpack
        that are parallelized with OpenMP . 

 - sjpack-test: Test programs for sjpack.
 
 - sjpack-cuda-test: Test programs for sjpack-cuda.

 - sjpack-mpi-test: Test programs for sjpack-mpi.

 Please refer to the MEMO file (sorry, written in Japanese) in each 
subdirectory for how to use.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Parallelization
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

 When you use packages parallelized with MPI (p3pack-mpi, snpack-mpi, 
sppack-mpi, and sopack-mpi, so far), please refer to manuals of compilers
and commands for MPI on the system. The sample programs (3D-box-mpi, 
non-div-2d-sphere-mpi, and shallow-water-sphere-mpi, so far) can be 
used as a reference for how to call the parallelized subroutines practically.

 In p3pack-mpi, the number of processes is assumed to be not greater than
1024. If the number of processes can be greater than 1024, change the value
of "MP" that is set in programs under the directory, p3pack-mpi/src/, as
      PARAMETER(MP=1024)
appropriately and remake the library.

 When you use packages parallelized with OpenMP (snpack, pzpack, and sjpack,
so far), please refer to manuals for how to set compiler options and
runtime environment variables on the system.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CUDA version
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

 A package, "sjpack-cuda", is provided as the CUDA version of a part
of transform routines in spherical harmonics transform package "sjpack"
to be used on GPGPUs made by Nvidia. Please refer to the document under
the directory, sjpack-cuda/doc/, for the details about the package.

 In the default settings, this sjpack-cuda package is not included in 
the library. If you want to use this package, you must make the library
after you set the values of NVCC and NVCFLAGS appropriately and add 
"sjpack-cuda" to the value of "DIRS" in Mkinclude.

